-- ditto.rofl — DITTO: pin the model, make the edit, prove what you promised
-- to preserve was preserved.
--
-- "This is a refactoring" is an assertion nobody checks. Tests check sampled
-- OUTPUTS on sampled INPUTS and everyone hopes that was enough. Nothing checks
-- STRUCTURE, and nothing lets the author say WHICH subset of the structure was
-- supposed to survive.
--
-- WHAT CANNOT BE PROMISED, SAID ONCE, HERE. Program equivalence is
-- undecidable. "Behaviour is identical" cannot be proved and promising it is a
-- lie. What can be proved is that the DECLARED AND EXTRACTABLE set of
-- invariants survived — strictly weaker than the informal promise and strictly
-- more useful, because it is checkable. Every verdict below is worded that way
-- and `undecidable/1` and `out_of_scope/1` are facts so the tool prints its own
-- boundary instead of implying it covered everything.
--
-- WHY THIS DOMAIN IS CHEAP. Extraction is deterministic at both ends, as in
-- examples/drip: the model of the code before the edit and the model after it
-- are both the output of the SAME dumb reader — scanners/js.ts, which already
-- exists in this repository and parses TypeScript into src_func / src_call /
-- src_import / src_export facts. Nobody judges anything on the way in. The one
-- thing a human must supply is the equivalence class, and that is the whole
-- point: declaring what counts as a refactoring is the human's job and cannot
-- be automated (the tool asserting into [claim] is `forged[audit]`, and
-- demo.ts fires it).
--
-- TWO LEDGERS FOR THE TWO MODELS, and that is the natural shape:
--   [before]  the frozen model: what the extractor read at the pinned
--             revision. Written by the freeze, which is a stored artefact.
--   [after]   the same extractor, re-run on the working tree.
--   [claim]   the engineer's declaration: which edits these are, which names
--             and paths were deliberately moved, what the entry points are.
--   [main]    every conclusion, reading all three through declared imports.
--
-- These are LEDGERS, not statuses (docs/choosing-perspectives.md). "Preserved"
-- and "violated" are DERIVED in [main]; nothing is ever filed under them. The
-- closest this comes to the perspective-per-run anti-pattern is worth naming:
-- [before] and [after] are not one ledger per extractor execution. There are
-- exactly two, always, by construction — the pinned baseline and the current
-- tree — and the next edit re-freezes [after] as the new [before] rather than
-- spawning a third. test/example-ditto.test.ts pins the count at two.
--
-- Fixture: examples/ditto/before/ and examples/ditto/after/, seven ordinary
-- TypeScript files each, carrying five declared edits (an extraction, a
-- rename, a module move, a data-structure swap and a de-duplication), one
-- planted defect and one planted loss of redundancy. README.md says which.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. the ledgers, and who may write into them
-- =========================================================================

authority(before, frozen_extract).
authority(after,  live_extract).
authority(claim,  engineer).
imports(main, before).
imports(main, after).
imports(main, claim).

-- And the other direction, which the leak audit asks about since `flows_to`
-- closed the flow graph. Every audit rule in boot.rofl reads [main] and
-- writes [audit], so content that reached [main] from one of these three
-- books reaches [audit] on the next hop. Each line below is a decision, not
-- a formality: yes, the audit ledger may name the frozen extraction, the
-- live one, and the engineer's claim -- reporting on them is the whole
-- reason this program exists, and `forged[audit]` cannot name a facts's
-- book without reading that book.
imports(audit, before).
imports(audit, after).
imports(audit, claim).

-- And the crossing `imports` cannot express, said in the one place it is
-- true. The extraction rules are polymorphic in the side -- `file[S](P, H)
-- :- src_file[S](P, H)` and its siblings serve [before] and [after] both,
-- which is the point, since two rules would be two chances to extract the
-- two sides differently -- and the comparison rules read [S] and write
-- [main]. So [main] gathers from a ledger it does not name, and no
-- `imports(main, From)` can say so: `imports` relates two REGISTERED
-- perspectives and `$var("S")` has no `authority` fact and never will.
-- `collects(main)` says it once, at the ledger that does the gathering, and
-- `collected[audit](main)` then reports that the declaration was exercised.
collects(main).

side(before).
side(after).

-- The extractor's own vocabulary. Declared, not merely populated: these facts
-- arrive from demo.ts after this file loads, and boot.rofl's
-- `undefined_premise` audit reads an unpopulated positive premise as a
-- misspelling — correctly. Declaring the input is the remedy and doubles as
-- the schema (same reason examples/moot declares its config schema).
edb(imports).
edb(src_file).      -- src_file[S](Path, Hash12)
edb(src_func).      -- src_func[S](Path, Name, Line)
edb(src_call).      -- src_call[S](Path, Caller, CalleeName)
edb(src_import).    -- src_import[S](Path, Specifier)
edb(src_export).    -- src_export[S](Path, Name)
edb(src_class).     -- src_class[S](Path, Name, Line)
edb(src_method).    -- src_method[S](Path, Class, Name, Line)
edb(src_parse_error).
edb(resolves).      -- resolves[S](Path, Specifier, TargetModule)
edb(extractor).     -- extractor[S](Tool, Version)

-- The engineer's declaration.
edb(edit).          -- edit[claim](Id, Kind)
edb(edit_note).     -- edit_note[claim](Id, Prose)
edb(renamed).       -- renamed[claim](OldName, NewName)
edb(moved).         -- moved[claim](OldPath, NewPath)
edb(entry_point).   -- entry_point[claim](fn(Path, Name))

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. the extractor is a node of the graph, and skew REFUSES the answer
-- =========================================================================
-- Freezing with one parser and checking with another is a source of both
-- false violations and false calm, and it is invisible unless the tool is made
-- to look. So the tool version is data, the mismatch is derived, and the
-- comparison is REFUSED rather than annotated: `checked/1` is what every
-- verdict below is guarded by, and it is empty while anything is refused.
--
-- A gate that has never said no is indistinguishable from an absent one, so
-- demo.ts §8 runs a world whose [after] was extracted by a different version
-- and shows the whole report collapse to REFUSED.

skew(Tool, V1, V2)    :- extractor[before](Tool, V1), extractor[after](Tool, V2), V1 != V2.
tool_swap(T1, T2)     :- extractor[before](T1, _), extractor[after](T2, _), T1 != T2.
any_before(yes)       :- extractor[before](_, _).
no_baseline(after)    :- extractor[after](_, _), not any_before(_).
refused(version_skew) :- skew(_, _, _).
refused(tool_swap)    :- tool_swap(_, _).
refused(no_baseline)  :- no_baseline(_).

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. the two models, lifted out of their ledgers into one vocabulary
-- =========================================================================
-- One rule per relation, with the LEDGER IN THE PERSPECTIVE VARIABLE and the
-- side carried into [main] as an ordinary column. The head perspective is
-- written out so the kernel emits `bridge_decl` and the cross-ledger read is
-- licensed rather than silent; examples/loot reads its rulepacks the same way.
--
-- The side is a column and not a perspective ONCE IT IS IN [main], and that is
-- deliberate: [main] is the book of this program's conclusions, and "which
-- revision was this observed in" is an attribute of the observation, not a
-- second book. The books are [before] and [after]; nothing writes into them
-- here.

file[main](S, P)          :- src_file[S](P, _), side(S).
content[main](S, P, H)    :- src_file[S](P, H), side(S).
func[main](S, P, F)       :- src_func[S](P, F, _), side(S).
exported[main](S, P, N)   :- src_export[S](P, N), side(S).
calls[main](S, P, F, C)   :- src_call[S](P, F, C), side(S).
imports_spec[main](S, P, Spec) :- src_import[S](P, Spec), side(S).
resolved[main](S, P, Spec, Q)  :- resolves[S](P, Spec, Q), side(S).

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. resolution — from a callee NAME to a definition site
-- =========================================================================
-- scanners/js.ts records a call as the enclosing function plus the callee's
-- syntactic name; it does not resolve the name to a file, because a scanner
-- that resolved imports would be a compiler. Resolution therefore happens in
-- RULES, which is where examples/moot puts its operator semantics for the same
-- reason: the reader stays dumb and the meaning stays inspectable.
--
-- `visible` is computed over every (module, name) pair rather than only over
-- names actually called, so `target` has exactly ONE derivation per pair. That
-- is not tidiness: the counting fold in §6 multiplies through this relation,
-- and examples/drip paid for learning that a helper with N derivations
-- multiplies every count downstream by N.

imports_edge[main](S, P, Q) :- imports_spec(S, P, Spec), resolved(S, P, Spec, Q).
visible[main](S, P, N, Q)   :- imports_edge(S, P, Q), func(S, Q, N), exported(S, Q, N).

target[main](S, P, N, P)    :- func(S, P, N).
target[main](S, P, N, Q)    :- visible(S, P, N, Q), not func(S, P, N).

-- Two modules exporting the same name into one importer. Not an error of the
-- code; an AMBIGUITY OF THIS MODEL, and the report prints it rather than
-- letting the extra derivation quietly double a count.
ambiguous(S, P, N)          :- target(S, P, N, Q), target(S, P, N, R), Q != R.

-- A node is a function AT a path. The pair is one term, so the call graph is
-- an ordinary binary relation and the path travels with the name — which is
-- exactly what a module move perturbs and what §5 canonicalises.
node[main](S, fn(P, F))                :- func(S, P, F).
edge[main](S, fn(P, F), fn(Q, N))      :- calls(S, P, F, N), func(S, P, F),
                                          target(S, P, N, Q).
has_caller[main](S, B)                 :- edge(S, _, B).

-- Everything the extractor saw called and could not place: library methods,
-- host builtins, anything reached through a value. This is the model's
-- coverage, printed as a number instead of assumed to be zero.
unresolved(S, P, F, N) :- calls(S, P, F, N), not target(S, P, N, _), not effect_api(N, _).

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. effects — the only semantics in the file, and it is a table
-- =========================================================================
-- An effect is a call into a declared external API. The table is the TOOL's
-- catalogue, not the engineer's claim: it must be identical on both sides by
-- construction, or the comparison would be measuring the classifier instead of
-- the code. That is the extractor-version invariant of §1 in miniature.

effect_api("db.insert",   db_write).
effect_api("db.update",   db_write).
effect_api("db.delete",   db_write).
effect_api("db.select",   db_read).
effect_api("http.post",   net_call).
effect_api("http.get",    net_call).
effect_api("mail.send",   mail_send).
effect_api("sink.append", audit_write).
effect_api("fs.writeFile", fs_write).

does[main](S, fn(P, F), E) :- calls(S, P, F, C), effect_api(C, E).

-- =========================================================================
-- 5. reachability, twice — once as a walk and once as simple paths
-- =========================================================================
-- The walk is what "can control get from here to there" means and it is the
-- right BOOLEAN. It is the wrong thing to COUNT: a recursive function puts the
-- graph on a cycle, every trip round the cycle is another derivation, and the
-- counting semiring answers "infinitely many" — honest, and useless as a
-- measure of redundancy. `flattenLines` in the fixture is recursive precisely
-- so that this is exercised rather than avoided.
--
-- So routes carry the visited chain INSIDE THE FACT, the technique
-- examples/nope uses for assume-role chains and examples/drip for metric
-- lineage. `fresh` is positive, not a negation: the chain domain is generated
-- by `chain` from the routes already derived, so this is ordinary monotone
-- recursion over terms and the stratification checker is never argued with.
-- The chain is keyed by SIDE as well as by path, or one path present in both
-- revisions would carry two derivations and double every count through it.

creach[main](S, A, B)     :- edge(S, A, B).
creach[main](S, A, C)     :- creach(S, A, B), edge(S, B, C).

chain[main](S, Path)      :- route(S, _, _, Path).
fresh[main](S, X, nil)    :- node(S, X).
fresh[main](S, X, cons(Y, Path)) :- chain(S, cons(Y, Path)), fresh(S, X, Path), X != Y.

route[main](S, A, A, cons(A, nil))   :- node(S, A).
route[main](S, A, C, cons(C, Path))  :- route(S, A, B, Path), edge(S, B, C),
                                        fresh(S, C, Path).

-- Reflexive by construction: a function that performs the effect itself
-- reaches it by the one-element route. The COUNT of this relation is the
-- number of independent routes from A to that effect, and §9 reads a drop in
-- it as lost redundancy rather than as a violation.
effect_reachable[main](S, A, E) :- route(S, A, B, _), does(S, B, E).

-- =========================================================================
-- 6. the declared identification — a rename and a move are NOT a diff
-- =========================================================================
-- A rename moves every extracted tuple that mentions the name; a module move
-- moves every tuple that mentions the path. A textual diff, a golden master or
-- a byte comparison therefore reports that everything changed, which is true
-- and worthless. What makes the comparison possible is the engineer DECLARING
-- the identification — and it has to be declared, because inferring it is
-- guessing (examples/drip offers exactly that guess and keeps it a guess).
--
-- The map applies to [before] only, never to [after]. Applying it in both
-- directions would silently rewrite an unrelated new function that happened to
-- reuse an old name, which is the sort of quiet wrongness this whole example
-- exists to make loud.

name[main](S, N)  :- func(S, _, N).
name[main](S, N)  :- exported(S, P, N), not func(S, P, N).
path[main](S, P)  :- file(S, P).
path[main](S, Q)  :- imports_edge(S, _, Q), not file(S, Q).

cname[main](before, N, M) :- renamed[claim](N, M).
cname[main](before, N, N) :- name(before, N), not renamed[claim](N, _).
cname[main](after,  N, N) :- name(after, N).
cpath[main](before, P, Q) :- moved[claim](P, Q).
cpath[main](before, P, P) :- path(before, P), not moved[claim](P, _).
cpath[main](after,  P, P) :- path(after, P).

cnode[main](S, fn(P, F), fn(Q, G)) :- node(S, fn(P, F)), cpath(S, P, Q), cname(S, F, G).
cedge[main](S, CA, CB)             :- edge(S, A, B), cnode(S, A, CA), cnode(S, B, CB).

-- The entry points are declared in the CANONICAL (post-edit) vocabulary and
-- mapped back onto each side. If an entry point stops existing, this relation
-- is empty on that side and every one of its observations is lost at once —
-- loudly, which is the correct behaviour for deleting the front door.
entry[main](S, A) :- entry_point[claim](CA), cnode(S, A, CA).

-- =========================================================================
-- 7. the invariant vocabulary — a set of derived predicates, not a file list
-- =========================================================================
-- Each invariant is a SET OF GROUND TUPLES, extracted the same way on both
-- sides. `obs(S, I, T)` is "revision S exhibits tuple T of invariant I", and
-- the whole check is set difference over it.
--
-- Four are canonical (the declared rename and move are applied first, so a
-- legal identification does not read as a change). Two are RAW on purpose:
-- `identifier` and `layout` are precisely the things the maps are allowed to
-- move, so canonicalising them would make a rename unfalsifiable.

invariant(entry_effect).  -- (entry point, effect) reachable from it
invariant(entry_reach).   -- (entry point, exported function) it can reach
invariant(boundary).      -- (module, exported name): the signature surface
invariant(module_dep).    -- (module, module): the import graph
invariant(internal).      -- (caller, callee): the call graph itself
invariant(identifier).    -- every name the extractor saw, raw
invariant(layout).        -- every file path, raw

-- Exact means the tuple set must be EQUAL. The others are no-loss: nothing may
-- disappear, and an addition is not a violation. Gaining an effect reachable
-- from an entry point IS a behaviour change, so entry_effect is exact.
exact_invariant(entry_effect).
exact_invariant(boundary).
exact_invariant(module_dep).

obs[main](S, entry_effect, pair(CA, E)) :- entry(S, A), effect_reachable(S, A, E),
                                           cnode(S, A, CA).
obs[main](S, entry_reach, pair(CA, CB)) :- entry(S, A), creach(S, A, B),
                                           boundary_node(S, B),
                                           cnode(S, A, CA), cnode(S, B, CB).
obs[main](S, boundary, pair(CP, CN))    :- exported(S, P, N), cpath(S, P, CP),
                                           cname(S, N, CN).
obs[main](S, module_dep, pair(CP, CQ))  :- imports_edge(S, P, Q), cpath(S, P, CP),
                                           cpath(S, Q, CQ).
obs[main](S, internal, pair(CA, CB))    :- cedge(S, CA, CB).
obs[main](S, identifier, N)             :- name(S, N).
obs[main](S, layout, P)                 :- file(S, P).

boundary_node[main](S, fn(P, N)) :- exported(S, P, N), func(S, P, N).

lost(I, T)   :- obs(before, I, T), not obs(after, I, T).
gained(I, T) :- obs(after, I, T), not obs(before, I, T).

-- =========================================================================
-- 8. the equivalence class — the catalogue is the tool's, the choice is not
-- =========================================================================
-- The catalogue below is fixed by the tool: it is what the WORDS mean. Which
-- of them applies to this edit is the engineer's declaration in [claim], and
-- that is the manual step the spec insists on and this file enforces
-- mechanically: the extractor has no authority over [claim].

class_preserves(extract_method, entry_effect).
class_preserves(extract_method, entry_reach).
class_preserves(extract_method, boundary).
class_preserves(extract_method, module_dep).
class_preserves(extract_method, layout).
class_permits(extract_method, internal).
class_permits(extract_method, identifier).

class_preserves(rename, entry_effect).
class_preserves(rename, entry_reach).
class_preserves(rename, boundary).
class_preserves(rename, module_dep).
class_preserves(rename, internal).
class_preserves(rename, layout).
class_permits(rename, identifier).

class_preserves(module_move, entry_effect).
class_preserves(module_move, entry_reach).
class_preserves(module_move, boundary).
class_preserves(module_move, module_dep).
class_preserves(module_move, internal).
class_preserves(module_move, identifier).
class_permits(module_move, layout).

class_preserves(data_structure, entry_effect).
class_preserves(data_structure, entry_reach).
class_preserves(data_structure, boundary).
class_preserves(data_structure, module_dep).
class_preserves(data_structure, layout).
class_permits(data_structure, internal).
class_permits(data_structure, identifier).

class_preserves(dedupe, entry_effect).
class_preserves(dedupe, entry_reach).
class_preserves(dedupe, boundary).
class_preserves(dedupe, module_dep).
class_preserves(dedupe, layout).
class_permits(dedupe, internal).
class_permits(dedupe, identifier).

-- MIXING EDITS WEAKENS THE CLAIM, MONOTONICALLY, and this is the honest
-- semantics of putting several refactorings in one change: you may only
-- promise what ALL of them promise. One permissive edit waives an invariant
-- for the whole comparison, and `weakened_by` names which edit cost which
-- promise so the price of a mixed change is a number rather than a feeling.

declared_kind(K)    :- edit[claim](_, K).
waived(I)           :- declared_kind(K), class_permits(K, I).
obliged(I)          :- declared_kind(K), class_preserves(K, I), not waived(I).
weakened_by(I, K2, K) :- declared_kind(K2), class_preserves(K2, I),
                         declared_kind(K), class_permits(K, I).

-- Nothing is checked while the comparison is refused. This is the whole point
-- of §1: a version-skewed report must not look like a clean one.
checked(I) :- obliged(I), not refused(_).

-- =========================================================================
-- 9. the verdicts
-- =========================================================================
-- The wording matters and is fixed here rather than in the renderer: what is
-- established is that the DECLARED AND EXTRACTABLE tuple set survived. Never
-- that behaviour is preserved.

violated(I, T, disappeared) :- checked(I), lost(I, T).
violated(I, T, appeared)    :- checked(I), gained(I, T), exact_invariant(I).
broken(I)                   :- violated(I, _, _).
held(I)                     :- checked(I), not broken(I).

-- Changed, and allowed to have changed. Counting these is what separates "the
-- edit did nothing" from "the edit did a great deal, all of it inside the
-- licence it declared".
permitted_change(I, T, removed) :- waived(I), lost(I, T).
permitted_change(I, T, added)   :- waived(I), gained(I, T).

-- LOST REDUNDANCY. The invariant still holds and the number of independent
-- routes that carry it has dropped. Not a violation — a future regression,
-- visible today. The counting semiring supplies the numbers (demo.ts §6); this
-- relation is the set the numbers are read over.
carried(I, T) :- obs(before, I, T), obs(after, I, T).

-- =========================================================================
-- 10. WHY it broke — a hypothesis, and the proof that it stays one
-- =========================================================================
-- whynot already walks the chain link by link; this is the one-sentence guess
-- that goes on the ticket. It is a GUESS: a call that used to exist does not,
-- and its target is now called from nowhere. Nothing in §7, §8 or §9 reads any
-- relation defined here, and demo.ts proves that from the kernel's own rule
-- dependency graph rather than asking to be believed — the same discipline
-- examples/drip applies to its rename hypothesis.

orphan(S, B)         :- boundary_node(S, B), not has_caller(S, B).
lost_edge(CA, CB)    :- cedge(before, CA, CB), not cedge(after, CA, CB).
carries_effect(CB, E) :- cnode(before, B, CB), does(before, B, E).

-- Three tiers of evidence for the same guess, each a separate derivation, so
-- the Viterbi fold in demo.ts takes the best-supported one rather than the
-- product of all of them.
hint(CA, CB, edge_gone)        :- lost_edge(CA, CB).
hint(CA, CB, edge_gone_orphan) :- lost_edge(CA, CB), orphan(after, CB).
hint(CA, CB, edge_gone_effect) :- lost_edge(CA, CB), orphan(after, CB),
                                  carries_effect(CB, _).
probable_cause(CA, CB) :- hint(CA, CB, _).

-- =========================================================================
-- 11. the boundary, as facts rather than as a paragraph
-- =========================================================================

undecidable(program_equivalence).
scope_covered(call_graph).
scope_covered(reachability_from_entry_points).
scope_covered(effects_through_declared_apis).
scope_covered(export_surface).
scope_covered(module_dependency).
out_of_scope(dynamic_dispatch).
out_of_scope(higher_order_callbacks).
out_of_scope(reflection_and_eval).
out_of_scope(control_flow_and_data_values).
out_of_scope(concurrency_and_ordering).
out_of_scope(performance_and_resource_use).
out_of_scope(anything_needing_symbolic_execution).

-- Acceptance scenario (examples/ditto/demo.ts runs all of it):
--   ? obliged(I) / waived(I)           -> what the declared class demands
--   ? held(I) vs violated(I, T, D)     -> 36 of 38 tuples, one defect, two rows
--   why  effect_reachable(before, ...) -> the chain that carried the effect
--   whynot effect_reachable(after, ..) -> the link that is no longer there
--   ? probable_cause(A, B)             -> a guess, and it reaches no verdict
--   counting over effect_reachable     -> an invariant that lost its spare route
--   excise src_call[before](...)       -> the same violation, computed a second way
--   a skewed extractor                 -> the whole report REFUSED
